  "description": "Path grammar vectors (write-time rules). normalize: valid inputs give the canonical NFC output; invalid inputs give the error kind of the first failing rule (rule order: UTF-8/non-empty; absolute; then the first offending character from left to right: backslash, control_char (C0, DEL, C1), noncharacter (U+FDD0-U+FDEF, U+xxFFFE, U+xxFFFF), disallowed_char (fixed list of bidi controls and invisible characters), unassigned_char (Unicode 15.0.0 Cn); NFC; empty/dot segments; segment and path byte limits). Lengths are counted in bytes of the NFC form. Inputs that cannot be written as JSON strings use input_hex. fold: the case-fold key NFD(casefold(NFD(input))), the canonical caseless match of Unicode 3.13 (D145) with the full case folding of CaseFolding.txt (statuses C and F); siblings: names that cannot coexist in one directory. stored_names: the read-time rules applied to names inside stored tree nodes; they are structural (valid UTF-8, non-empty, no '/', no backslash, no control characters, no noncharacters, not '.'/'..', at most 255 bytes) and do not depend on Unicode tables (no NFC, case-fold, unassigned or disallowed-list checks); they give no output, only acceptance or the error kind. Long inputs use a generator: segments joined by '/', each segment = unit repeated `repeat` times, emitted `times` times (default 1). Cases with ucd_version depend on the Unicode version: they hold for V = 15.0.0 only (assigned or not), and a checker whose Unicode tables have another version must skip them. Cases with multiple_violations break more than one rule: their error kind is the one the reference implementation reports (the first failing step, characters from left to right) and is not normative; another implementation may report any of the violated kinds. For every other case the error kind is normative.",
